\def\title{RENDER}

@i ../../types.gw

@* Introduction.
Some years ago I wrote mpresty, a small web application on OpenResty, which
is nginx with LuaJIT inside. An author writes MetaPost or Graphviz source
straight into an HTML page,
$$\vbox{\halign{\.{#}\hfil\cr
<metapost width="200">\cr
beginfig(1)\cr
\ \ draw fullcircle scaled 2cm;\cr
endfig;\cr
</metapost>\cr}}$$
and on the way out mpresty runs the engine and replaces the element by an
\.{<img>} pointing at the resulting SVG. The same works for an ordinary
\.{<img src="tree.mp">}, where the extension of the source file chooses the
engine. The original lives at \.{https://github.com/sjnam/mpresty}.

In August 2026 I ported it to \GO/, and this package is the heart of the port:
it finds the graphics elements in a parsed document, renders them, in parallel,
and rewrites them in place. The Lua code it replaces was spread over
\.{lib/mpresty/*.lua}, one file per engine plus a \.{base.lua} they shared.

@ One thing in the Lua code could not be carried over as it was, and it shaped
everything here. \.{base.lua} stored the per-request state, the command to run,
the document, whether to use the cache, on the module itself, while several
light threads (\.{ngx.thread.spawn}) walked the document at once. That was safe
only because those threads are coroutines: cooperative, and all on one
operating-system thread, so nobody could change the module's fields between
two statements of somebody else. Goroutines enjoy no such protection.

So in this package a |Renderer| is immutable configuration, shared freely by
every request, and everything that varies from one node to the next travels as
a local variable of the goroutine that renders it. The |Processor| holds only
configuration and a few concurrency-safe helpers, so a single one serves the
whole server.

@ The skeleton of the package.
@c
// Package render turns graphics source embedded in an HTML document
// into SVG images.
package render

import (
	"bytes"
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"hash/crc32"
	"io"
	"log/slog"
	"net/http"
	"os"
	"os/exec"
	"path"
	"path/filepath"
	"runtime"
	"slices"
	"strconv"
	"strings"
	"time"

	"golang.org/x/sync/errgroup"
	"golang.org/x/sync/singleflight"

	"github.com/sjnam/go-mpresty/internal/cache"
	"github.com/sjnam/go-mpresty/internal/htmldoc"
)

@<Renderers@>
@<Updaters@>
@<The processor@>
@<Functions@>

@* Renderers.
A |Renderer| describes one graphics engine: the element it claims, the
extension of its source files, the command that runs it, and the text to wrap
around the author's source. The Lua original built the command line as a
string and handed it to a shell, with the \.{cmd} attribute of the element
pasted in. Here |Command| returns an argument vector that runs without a shell,
so whatever an author writes in \.{cmd} reaches the engine as one harmless
word; and the list |Commands| bounds even that.
@<Renderers@>=
// Renderer describes one graphics engine. Every field is read-only once
// the Renderer is registered, so a single value is shared safely.
type Renderer struct {
	Tag       string   // custom element name, e.g. "metapost"
	Ext       string   // source file extension, e.g. "mp"
	Cmd       string   // default command
	Commands  []string // commands a cmd attribute may select
	Preamble  string
	Postamble string

	// Command returns the argv to run inside a job directory that already
	// holds fname.Ext. It must leave fname.svg there.
	Command func(cmd, fname string) []string
}

@ MetaPost needs to be told to write SVG, and under which name. The output
template \.{\%j.svg} names the file after the job, which is the source file's
name without extension. In SVG mode \.{prologues:=3} converts the glyphs of
the labels to path definitions at the top of the file, so the picture looks
the same whatever fonts the browser has (see section~8.1 of the MetaPost
manual). The Lua version appended \.{end} to every source, and so do I, but on
a line of its own so that a source ending in a comment still terminates.
@<Renderers@>=
// MetaPost renders metapost elements and .mp sources.
var MetaPost = &Renderer{
	Tag:      "metapost",
	Ext:      "mp",
	Cmd:      "mpost",
	Commands: []string{"mpost", "mpost-lua"},
	Preamble: "prologues:=3;\noutputtemplate:=\"%j.svg\";\noutputformat:=\"svg\";\n",
	Postamble: "\nend\n",
	Command: func(cmd, fname string) []string {
		return []string{cmd, "-interaction=nonstopmode", fname + ".mp"}
	},
}

@ Graphviz is simpler: every layout engine in the family takes the same
options, so an author may pick any of them with \.{cmd}.
@<Renderers@>=
// Graphviz renders graphviz elements and .gv sources.
var Graphviz = &Renderer{
	Tag: "graphviz",
	Ext: "gv",
	Cmd: "dot",
	Commands: []string{
		"dot", "neato", "fdp", "sfdp", "twopi", "circo", "osage", "patchwork",
	},
	Command: func(cmd, fname string) []string {
		return []string{cmd, "-Tsvg", fname + ".gv", "-o", fname + ".svg"}
	},
}

@* Updaters.
Once a node's SVG exists, something must rewrite the node. In mpresty that
something was a Lua function called |fn_update_node|, and a page could supply
its own: \.{exemples.html} showed the source above each picture, for instance.
The convention was that |fn_update_node| might be a single function or a table
of functions keyed by tag name. In \GO/ the function is an |UpdateNodeFunc|,
and |Updaters| holds both forms at once. The arguments are the document, the
element that carried the source, the URI of the SVG, and the graphics source
itself.
@<Updaters@>=
// UpdateNodeFunc rewrites one graphics node once its image is ready.
type UpdateNodeFunc func(doc, node *htmldoc.Node, uri, content string)

// Updaters selects an UpdateNodeFunc per tag: an entry of ByTag if there
// is one, else Default, else DefaultUpdateNode.
type Updaters struct {
	Default UpdateNodeFunc
	ByTag   map[string]UpdateNodeFunc
}

@ The default rewrite is the one in \.{base.lua}: the node becomes an
\.{<img>}, keeps whatever attributes the author gave it, and gets a width of
300 pixels unless the author gave one.
@<Functions@>=
// DefaultUpdateNode replaces the node with an img pointing at the SVG.
func DefaultUpdateNode(doc, node *htmldoc.Node, uri, content string) {
	htmldoc.Rename(node, "img")
	htmldoc.SetAttr(node, "src", uri)
	if !htmldoc.HasAttr(node, "width") {
		htmldoc.SetAttr(node, "width", "300")
	}
}

@* The processor.
A |Processor| is configured once, from |Options|, and then renders any number
of documents, concurrently. Every option has a sensible default, so the zero
|Options| with only |Root| filled in is a working configuration.
@<The processor@>=
// Options configures a Processor.
type Options struct {
	Root      string        // document root; sources are read under it
	WorkDir   string        // rendered SVGs, relative to Root
	Timeout   time.Duration // wall clock budget for one engine run
	MaxJobs   int           // concurrent engine runs
	Cache     *cache.Cache  // caches remote sources
	Client    *http.Client  // fetches remote sources
	Renderers []*Renderer   // the engines
	Logger    *slog.Logger
}

@ The processor keeps its renderers twice, by tag for the custom elements and
by extension for the \.{<img>} form. The |singleflight.Group| makes identical
concurrent renders collapse into one engine run; more of it later.
@<The processor@>=
// Processor renders every graphics node in a document. It is safe for
// concurrent use and holds no per-request state.
type Processor struct {
	root    string
	workDir string
	tmpDir  string
	timeout time.Duration
	maxJobs int
	cache   *cache.Cache
	client  *http.Client
	log     *slog.Logger

	byTag map[string]*Renderer
	byExt map[string]*Renderer
	group singleflight.Group
}

@ The constructor fills in the defaults, builds the processor, and makes sure
the directory where the engines will work exists.
@<Functions@>=
// New prepares a Processor, creating the work directory if needed.
func New(opts Options) (*Processor, error) {
	root, err := filepath.Abs(opts.Root)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	@<Fill in the defaults of |opts|@>
	@<Create the processor |p|@>
	if err := os.MkdirAll(p.tmpDir, 0o755); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	@<Register the renderers of |opts| with |p|@>
	return p, nil
}

@ The images go to \.{images/}, as they did in the Lua version. An engine run
gets thirty seconds, which is generous for any figure a person draws by hand;
there may be as many runs at once as there are processors to run them; and a
remote source gets fifteen seconds to arrive.
@<Fill in the defaults of |opts|@>=
if opts.WorkDir == "" {
	opts.WorkDir = "images"
}
if opts.Timeout <= 0 {
	opts.Timeout = 30 * time.Second
}
if opts.MaxJobs <= 0 {
	opts.MaxJobs = runtime.GOMAXPROCS(0)
}
if opts.Cache == nil {
	opts.Cache = cache.New(0)
}
if opts.Client == nil {
	opts.Client = &http.Client{Timeout: 15 * time.Second}
}
if opts.Renderers == nil {
	opts.Renderers = []*Renderer{MetaPost, Graphviz}
}
if opts.Logger == nil {
	opts.Logger = slog.Default()
}

@ Engine jobs run in scratch directories under \.{images/.tmp}. Putting them
under the work directory, rather than in the system's temporary directory,
guarantees that the finished SVG lies on the same file system as its final
home, so moving it there is a rename and never a copy.
@<Create the processor |p|@>=
p := &Processor{
	root:    root,
	workDir: opts.WorkDir,
	tmpDir:  filepath.Join(root, filepath.FromSlash(opts.WorkDir), ".tmp"),
	timeout: opts.Timeout,
	maxJobs: opts.MaxJobs,
	cache:   opts.Cache,
	client:  opts.Client,
	log:     opts.Logger,
	byTag:   make(map[string]*Renderer),
	byExt:   make(map[string]*Renderer),
}

@ An engine that is not installed still renders---as a red error on the page.
It is kinder to say so once at startup than to let the first author find out.
@<Register the renderers of |opts| with |p|@>=
for _, r := range opts.Renderers {
	p.byTag[r.Tag] = r
	p.byExt[r.Ext] = r
	if _, err := exec.LookPath(r.Cmd); err != nil {
		p.log.Warn("engine not installed; its elements will render as errors",
			"tag", r.Tag, "cmd", r.Cmd)
	}
}

@ The parser in package |htmldoc| must be told which elements hold graphics
source, so that it can shield their bodies. Those are exactly the tags of the
registered renderers.
@<Functions@>=
// RawTags lists the element names whose bodies must reach the engine
// verbatim. Pass them to htmldoc.Parse.
func (p *Processor) RawTags() []string {
	tags := make([]string, 0, len(p.byTag))
	for tag := range p.byTag {
		tags = append(tags, tag)
	}
	return tags
}

@ What varies from one page to the next is gathered in a |Request|. The flag
|NoCache| is the old \.{?debug} query, which made the Lua version render
everything afresh.
@<The processor@>=
// Request carries everything that varies from one page render to the next.
type Request struct {
	// BaseURI is the URI of the page being rendered; relative src
	// attributes resolve against its directory.
	BaseURI string
	// NoCache renders every node afresh.
	NoCache bool
	// Updaters chooses how each rendered node is rewritten.
	Updaters Updaters
}

@* Processing a document.
Here is the method the server calls, once per page. It finds every node that a
renderer claims, starts a goroutine for each, and waits for all of them.

A node that fails to render is replaced by its error message rather than
failing the whole page; a page with one broken figure out of thirty is still
worth reading. So the goroutines report only one kind of error to the group:
cancellation, when the client has gone away and there is no page to finish.
The |errgroup| then cancels the rest.
@<Functions@>=
// Process renders every graphics node in doc, in parallel, and rewrites
// it in place. A node that fails to render shows its error instead; only
// cancellation is reported as an error.
func (p *Processor) Process(ctx context.Context, doc *htmldoc.Node, req Request) error {
	@<Collect the |jobs| in |doc|@>
	if len(jobs) == 0 {
		return nil
	}
	@<Let |base| be the directory of the page@>
	g, ctx := errgroup.WithContext(ctx)
	g.SetLimit(p.maxJobs)
	for _, j := range jobs {
		g.Go(func() error {
			@<Render the node of |j| and rewrite it@>
			return nil
		})
	}
	return g.Wait()
}

@ A |job| pairs a node with the renderer that claimed it.
@<The processor@>=
type job struct {
	r    *Renderer
	node *htmldoc.Node
}

@ A renderer claims its custom elements by tag name, and every \.{<img>} whose
\.{src} ends in its extension. The list is collected in full before any job
starts, so the goroutines can rewrite their nodes without upsetting the
search.
@<Collect the |jobs| in |doc|@>=
var jobs []job
for tag, r := range p.byTag {
	for _, n := range htmldoc.Elements(doc, tag) {
		jobs = append(jobs, job{r: r, node: n})
	}
}
for _, n := range htmldoc.Elements(doc, "img") {
	if src, ok := htmldoc.Attr(n, "src"); ok {
		if r, ok := p.byExt[strings.TrimPrefix(path.Ext(src), ".")]; ok {
			jobs = append(jobs, job{r: r, node: n})
		}
	}
}

@ A relative \.{src} resolves against the directory of the page, as a browser
would resolve it. I keep that directory with its trailing slash, so that
resolving is plain concatenation.
@<Let |base| be the directory of the page@>=
base := path.Dir(req.BaseURI)
if base != "/" {
	base += "/"
}

@* Rendering one node.
Each goroutine walks its node through four steps: decide whether the cache may
be used, get the graphics source, choose the command, and make sure the SVG
exists. Any step may fail. Rather than return from the middle, each step sets
|err| and the later ones stand aside, so that the outcome is decided in one
place at the end: the error shown on the page, or the image.

The three strings are declared up front because the steps share them: the
source in |content|, the command in |cmd|, and the URI of the image in |uri|.
@<Render the node of |j| and rewrite it@>=
r, node := j.r, j.node
var content, cmd, uri string
var err error
@<Decide whether |node| may use the cache@>
@<Get the graphics source of |node| into |content|@>
if err == nil {
	@<Choose the command |cmd|, if |r| allows it@>
}
if err == nil {
	@<Make sure the SVG for |content| exists, and set |uri|@>
}
if err != nil {
	@<Show |err| in red where the image would have been@>
} else {
	@<Rewrite |node| with the updater for its tag@>
}

@ Two things switch the cache off: the request as a whole, through
\.{?debug}, and the element itself, through \.{cache="no"}. Like every
attribute the pipeline consumes, \.{cache} is removed from the node, so it
does not leak into the page.
@<Decide whether |node| may use the cache@>=
useCache := !req.NoCache
if htmldoc.AttrOr(node, "cache", "") == "no" {
	useCache = false
}
htmldoc.RemoveAttr(node, "cache")

@ The source is the body of the element, unless the element has a \.{src}
attribute, in which case it is the file that attribute names. That file may
be local, under the document root, or remote.
@<Get the graphics source of |node| into |content|@>=
if src, ok := htmldoc.Attr(node, "src"); !ok {
	content = htmldoc.TextContent(node)
} else {
	htmldoc.RemoveAttr(node, "src")
	if strings.HasPrefix(src, "http://") || strings.HasPrefix(src, "https://") {
		@<Fetch the remote source |src| into |content|@>
	} else {
		@<Read the local source |src| into |content|@>
	}
}

@ The Lua version fetched even a local source over HTTP, through nginx, with
|ngx.location.capture|. A subrequest is confined to what nginx serves, so it
could never wander out of the document root. Reading the file system directly
is much cheaper, but the confinement must now be done by hand, and that is the
business of |resolve|.
@<Read the local source |src| into |content|@>=
var name string
if name, err = p.resolve(src, base); err == nil {
	var b []byte
	if b, err = os.ReadFile(name); err == nil {
		content = string(b)
	} else {
		err = fmt.Errorf("read %s: %w", src, err)
	}
}

@ |path.Clean| removes every \.{..} that it can, and a rooted path has nothing
above its root, so cleaning \.{/../secret} yields \.{/secret}. A URI that
starts with a slash therefore cannot escape. A relative one is rooted by
|base|, which comes from the page's own URI and starts with a slash as well.
Still, I check the result instead of trusting that argument: a test calls
|resolve| with an empty |base| precisely to see the check refuse.
@<Functions@>=
// resolve maps a document-relative or absolute URI to a path inside the
// document root, refusing anything that would escape it.
func (p *Processor) resolve(uri, base string) (string, error) {
	if !strings.HasPrefix(uri, "/") {
		uri = base + uri
	}
	name := filepath.Join(p.root, filepath.FromSlash(path.Clean(uri)))
	if name != p.root && !strings.HasPrefix(name, p.root+string(os.PathSeparator)) {
		return "", fmt.Errorf("path %q escapes the document root", uri)
	}
	return name, nil
}

@ A remote source is cached under its URL, in the LRU of package |cache|, as
the Lua version cached it in its shared dictionary. Without that cache a page
showing a figure from another site would fetch it on every view, even though
the SVG itself is long since on disk.
@<Fetch the remote source |src| into |content|@>=
key := "src:" + src
hit := false
if useCache {
	content, hit = p.cache.Get(key)
}
if !hit {
	@<Download |src| into |content|@>
	if err == nil {
		p.cache.Set(key, content)
	}
}

@ The download uses the goroutine's context, so a client who goes away stops
it. Anything but \.{200 OK} is an error, and a source larger than four
megabytes is cut short; nobody writes a MetaPost figure that long, and the
limit keeps a hostile URL from filling memory.
@<Download |src| into |content|@>=
var hreq *http.Request
var res *http.Response
hreq, err = http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, http.MethodGet, src, nil)
if err == nil {
	res, err = p.client.Do(hreq)
}
if err == nil {
	if res.StatusCode != http.StatusOK {
		err = fmt.Errorf("fetch %s: %s", src, res.Status)
	} else if b, rerr := io.ReadAll(io.LimitReader(res.Body, 4<<20)); rerr != nil {
		err = fmt.Errorf("fetch %s: %w", src, rerr)
	} else {
		content = string(b)
	}
	res.Body.Close()
}

@ The command comes from the \.{cmd} attribute, if any, and must be the
renderer's default or on its list.
@<Choose the command |cmd|, if |r| allows it@>=
cmd = htmldoc.AttrOr(node, "cmd", r.Cmd)
htmldoc.RemoveAttr(node, "cmd")
if cmd != r.Cmd && !slices.Contains(r.Commands, cmd) {
	err = fmt.Errorf("%s: command %q is not allowed", r.Tag, cmd)
}

@* Running an engine.
The SVG is named after the command and a CRC-32 of the source, exactly as the
Lua version named it, so an \.{images/} directory filled by the original stays
valid after the port. The CRC is taken of the author's source alone, before
the preamble and postamble are wrapped around it.

The rendered file is its own cache. If it exists, we are done. The Lua
version remembered in its shared dictionary which sources it had rendered;
asking the file system instead cannot go stale when somebody empties
\.{images/}, and it survives a restart.

Two requests for the same source at the same moment, which is what happens
when a popular page is fresh, would both find no file and both run the
engine. The |singleflight.Group| collapses them: the second caller waits for
the first one's run and shares its outcome. Inside, the cache is checked a
second time, because the file may have appeared while this caller was on its
way to the group.
@<Make sure the SVG for |content| exists, and set |uri|@>=
fname := cmd + strconv.FormatUint(uint64(crc32.ChecksumIEEE([]byte(content))), 10)
uri = path.Join("/", p.workDir, fname+".svg")
if !useCache || !p.rendered(fname) {
	_, err, _ = p.group.Do(r.Tag+fname, func() (any, error) {
		if useCache && p.rendered(fname) {
			return nil, nil
		}
		@<Run |r| on |content| in a scratch directory and keep the SVG@>
	})
}

@ Two little functions name the file and ask whether it exists.
@<Functions@>=
// rendered reports whether the SVG for this name is already on disk.
func (p *Processor) rendered(fname string) bool {
	_, err := os.Stat(p.svgPath(fname))
	return err == nil
}

func (p *Processor) svgPath(fname string) string {
	return filepath.Join(p.root, filepath.FromSlash(p.workDir), fname+".svg")
}

@ Isolation matters more here than it did in Lua. The original ran every
engine in \.{images/} itself and cleaned up after it with
\.{rm F.log F.mp*}. But MetaPost's helper \.{makempx}, which typesets the
\.{btex} labels, writes temporary files with fixed names, so two genuinely
parallel runs in one directory would trample each other. Each run therefore
gets a scratch directory of its own, removed when the run is over, and only
the finished SVG leaves it. The run also gets its own deadline.

This code runs inside the closure given to |group.Do|, so its |return|
statements return from that closure, with the error the group hands on.
@<Run |r| on |content| in a scratch directory and keep the SVG@>=
ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, p.timeout)
defer cancel()
dir, err := os.MkdirTemp(p.tmpDir, "job-")
if err != nil {
	return nil, err
}
defer os.RemoveAll(dir)
text := r.Preamble + content + r.Postamble
if err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, fname+"."+r.Ext), []byte(text), 0o644); err != nil {
	return nil, err
}
@<Run the engine in |dir|@>
@<Move the SVG out of |dir|@>

@ An engine must never wait on a terminal, so its standard input is empty; and
its output and error streams go to one buffer, since they are interleaved in
the log a human will want to read.

The environment matters to \TeX-based engines. \.{TEXMFOUTPUT} and
\.{openout\_any=p} confine what they may write. The search paths deserve a
word of their own. A source may \.{input} a sibling: \.{benchmark.html}'s
\.{bird.mp} inputs \.{courbes.mp}. The Lua version got that for free by
running in the directory where the sources were; an isolated job needs a
search path that includes the document root. \.{TEXINPUTS} is there for
MetaPost's sake as well, because \.{btex} labels are typeset by \.{makempx},
which runs plain \TeX\ and searches along it.
@<Run the engine in |dir|@>=
argv := r.Command(cmd, fname)
c := exec.CommandContext(ctx, argv[0], argv[1:]...)
c.Dir = dir
c.Stdin = nil
c.Env = append(os.Environ(),
	"TEXMFOUTPUT="+dir,
	"openout_any=p",
	"MPINPUTS="+p.inputs(dir),
	"TEXINPUTS="+p.inputs(dir),
)
var out bytes.Buffer
c.Stdout, c.Stderr = &out, &out
if err := c.Run(); err != nil {
	if ctx.Err() != nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: %w", argv[0], ctx.Err())
	}
	diag := out.String()
	@<Trim |diag| to the part worth showing@>
	return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: %w\n%s", argv[0], err, diag)
}

@ The search path in kpathsea's notation: the job's own directory first, then
the document root and everything below it (a doubled separator asks kpathsea
to search recursively), then, by an empty element, whatever the installation
provides.
@<Functions@>=
// inputs is the kpathsea search path for a job.
func (p *Processor) inputs(dir string) string {
	sep := string(filepath.ListSeparator)
	sub := p.root + string(filepath.Separator) + string(filepath.Separator)
	return strings.Join([]string{dir, sub, ""}, sep)
}

@ The diagnostic will be shown on the page, so it should be short. MetaPost and
\TeX\ report the actual error at the end of a long log, so I keep the last
thirty lines. Some engines say nothing on their output streams and write
everything to a log file, which is then the better source.
@<Trim |diag| to the part worth showing@>=
if strings.TrimSpace(diag) == "" {
	if b, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(dir, fname+".log")); err == nil {
		diag = string(b)
	}
}
lines := strings.Split(strings.TrimRight(diag, "\n"), "\n")
if len(lines) > 30 {
	lines = append([]string{"..."}, lines[len(lines)-30:]...)
}
diag = strings.Join(lines, "\n")

@ An engine that exits happily without producing the SVG is an error too.
Otherwise the file moves to its final place, atomically, so no reader ever sees half an SVG.
@<Move the SVG out of |dir|@>=
svg := filepath.Join(dir, fname+".svg")
if _, err := os.Stat(svg); err != nil {
	return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s produced no %s.svg", cmd, fname)
}
return nil, os.Rename(svg, p.svgPath(fname))

@* Showing the result.
If |err| is set, the node becomes a red \.{<pre>} with the message in it, so
the author sees on the page itself what went wrong and where. Cancellation is
the exception: then nobody is reading the page, and the goroutine returns the
error so that the group stops the others.
@<Show |err| in red where the image would have been@>=
if ctx.Err() != nil {
	return ctx.Err()
}
p.log.Error("render failed", "tag", r.Tag, "uri", req.BaseURI, "err", err)
htmldoc.Rename(node, "pre")
htmldoc.SetTextContent(node, err.Error())
htmldoc.SetAttr(node, "style", "color:red")
htmldoc.RemoveAttr(node, "width")

@ Otherwise the source text goes, and the updater chosen for this tag rewrites
the node: the one registered for the tag, else the request's default, else
|DefaultUpdateNode|.
@<Rewrite |node| with the updater for its tag@>=
htmldoc.RemoveChildren(node)
update := DefaultUpdateNode
if fn := req.Updaters.ByTag[r.Tag]; fn != nil {
	update = fn
} else if req.Updaters.Default != nil {
	update = req.Updaters.Default
}
update(doc, node, uri, content)

@* Tests.
\.{GTANGLE} writes the tests to \.{render\_test.go}. Most of them use a fake
engine that needs no \TeX\ installation: its ``command'' is \.{cp}, which
copies the source file to the SVG the pipeline expects. So the tests exercise
the plumbing, not MetaPost, and the SVG they find is the very text the engine
was given, preamble and postamble included.
@(render_test.go@>=
package render

import (
	"context"
	"hash/crc32"
	"io"
	"log/slog"
	"os"
	"os/exec"
	"path/filepath"
	"strconv"
	"strings"
	"testing"

	"github.com/sjnam/go-mpresty/internal/htmldoc"
)

@<Test helpers@>

@ Every test makes its processor in a temporary directory of its own, with the
fake engine as its only renderer and a logger that says nothing.
@<Test helpers@>=
func newProcessor(t *testing.T, root string) *Processor {
	t.Helper()
	fake := &Renderer{
		Tag:       "fake",
		Ext:       "txt",
		Cmd:       "cp",
		Commands:  []string{"cp"},
		Preamble:  "P:",
		Postamble: ":Q",
		Command: func(cmd, fname string) []string {
			return []string{"cp", fname + ".txt", fname + ".svg"}
		},
	}
	p, err := New(Options{Root: root, Renderers: []*Renderer{fake}, Logger: quietLogger()})
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	return p
}

func quietLogger() *slog.Logger {
	return slog.New(slog.NewTextHandler(io.Discard, nil))
}

@ Then most tests parse a page, process it, and look at the tree.
@<Test helpers@>=
func process(t *testing.T, p *Processor, html string, req Request) *htmldoc.Node {
	t.Helper()
	doc, err := htmldoc.Parse(strings.NewReader(html), p.RawTags()...)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if req.BaseURI == "" {
		req.BaseURI = "/page.html"
	}
	if err := p.Process(context.Background(), doc, req); err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	return doc
}

@ The expected file names are computed the way the pipeline computes them:
from the CRC-32 of the source itself.
@<Test helpers@>=
func crcOf(s string) string {
	return strconv.FormatUint(uint64(crc32.ChecksumIEEE([]byte(s))), 10)
}

@ Files go in and out of the temporary root through two helpers that fail the
test rather than return an error.
@<Test helpers@>=
func writeFile(t *testing.T, name, content string) {
	t.Helper()
	if err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(name), 0o755); err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if err := os.WriteFile(name, []byte(content), 0o644); err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
}

func readFile(t *testing.T, name string) string {
	t.Helper()
	b, err := os.ReadFile(name)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	return string(b)
}

@ The first test follows one inline source all the way. The image must be
named by the CRC of \.{hello}, keep the author's width, and contain what the
engine saw: the source wrapped in preamble and postamble.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestRendersInlineSource(t *testing.T) {
	root := t.TempDir()
	p := newProcessor(t, root)
	doc := process(t, p, `<fake width="120">hello</fake>`, Request{})
	img := htmldoc.Elements(doc, "img")
	if len(img) != 1 {
		t.Fatalf("got %d img elements, want 1", len(img))
	}
	src, _ := htmldoc.Attr(img[0], "src")
	if want := "/images/cp" + crcOf("hello") + ".svg"; src != want {
		t.Errorf("src = %q, want %q", src, want)
	}
	if w, _ := htmldoc.Attr(img[0], "width"); w != "120" {
		t.Errorf("width = %q, want the author's 120", w)
	}
	got := readFile(t, filepath.Join(root, "images", "cp"+crcOf("hello")+".svg"))
	if got != "P:hello:Q" {
		t.Errorf("engine input = %q, want %q", got, "P:hello:Q")
	}
}

@ Without a width from the author, the default updater supplies 300.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestDefaultWidthWhenAuthorGivesNone(t *testing.T) {
	p := newProcessor(t, t.TempDir())
	doc := process(t, p, `<fake>x</fake>`, Request{})
	if w, _ := htmldoc.Attr(htmldoc.Elements(doc, "img")[0], "width"); w != "300" {
		t.Errorf("width = %q, want 300", w)
	}
}

@ The \.{<img src="....txt">} form must render just like the custom element,
and a relative \.{src} resolves against the directory of the page.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestRendersImgBySourceExtension(t *testing.T) {
	root := t.TempDir()
	writeFile(t, filepath.Join(root, "source", "a.txt"), "from-file")
	p := newProcessor(t, root)
	doc := process(t, p, `<img src="/source/a.txt" width="90">`, Request{})
	src, _ := htmldoc.Attr(htmldoc.Elements(doc, "img")[0], "src")
	if want := "/images/cp" + crcOf("from-file") + ".svg"; src != want {
		t.Errorf("src = %q, want %q", src, want)
	}
}

func TestRelativeSourceResolvesAgainstPage(t *testing.T) {
	root := t.TempDir()
	writeFile(t, filepath.Join(root, "docs", "b.txt"), "sibling")
	p := newProcessor(t, root)
	doc := process(t, p, `<img src="b.txt">`, Request{BaseURI: "/docs/index.html"})
	src, _ := htmldoc.Attr(htmldoc.Elements(doc, "img")[0], "src")
	if want := "/images/cp" + crcOf("sibling") + ".svg"; src != want {
		t.Errorf("src = %q, want %q", src, want)
	}
}

@ A traversing \.{src} must never reach outside the document root. I plant a
file next to the root and ask for it; cleaning the URI as a rooted path
neutralizes the \.{..}, so the file simply is not there, and the node turns
into an error that must not contain the planted text.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestTraversingSourceStaysInsideRoot(t *testing.T) {
	root := t.TempDir()
	writeFile(t, filepath.Join(filepath.Dir(root), "secret.txt"), "outside")
	p := newProcessor(t, root)
	doc := process(t, p, `<img src="/../secret.txt">`, Request{})
	pre := htmldoc.Elements(doc, "pre")
	if len(pre) != 1 {
		t.Fatalf("got %d error nodes, want 1", len(pre))
	}
	if got := htmldoc.TextContent(pre[0]); strings.Contains(got, "outside") {
		t.Errorf("the file outside the root was read: %q", got)
	}
	if n := len(htmldoc.Elements(doc, "img")); n != 0 {
		t.Errorf("got %d rendered images, want none", n)
	}
}

@ That test never reaches the check inside |resolve|, so this one calls it
directly, with a path that no cleaning can save.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestResolveRefusesEscape(t *testing.T) {
	p := newProcessor(t, t.TempDir())
	if _, err := p.resolve("../../etc/passwd", ""); err == nil {
		t.Error("resolve accepted a path that escapes the root")
	}
	if _, err := p.resolve("a.txt", "/docs/"); err != nil {
		t.Errorf("resolve rejected an ordinary path: %v", err)
	}
}

@ A \.{cmd} attribute picks the engine binary, so it must be bounded by the
renderer's list. The Lua version pasted it into a shell command.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestRejectsDisallowedCommand(t *testing.T) {
	p := newProcessor(t, t.TempDir())
	doc := process(t, p, `<fake cmd="rm">x</fake>`, Request{})
	pre := htmldoc.Elements(doc, "pre")
	if len(pre) != 1 {
		t.Fatalf("got %d error nodes, want 1", len(pre))
	}
	if got := htmldoc.TextContent(pre[0]); !strings.Contains(got, "not allowed") {
		t.Errorf("error text = %q", got)
	}
}

@ An SVG already on disk is reused untouched. I plant a sentinel where the
image belongs and check that it survives an ordinary request, and that both
\.{?debug} and \.{cache="no"} replace it.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestCacheReusesRenderedFile(t *testing.T) {
	root := t.TempDir()
	p := newProcessor(t, root)
	svg := filepath.Join(root, "images", "cp"+crcOf("keep")+".svg")
	writeFile(t, svg, "SENTINEL")
	process(t, p, `<fake>keep</fake>`, Request{})
	if got := readFile(t, svg); got != "SENTINEL" {
		t.Errorf("cached file was rebuilt: %q", got)
	}
	@<Check that |NoCache| and \.{cache="no"} rebuild |svg|@>
}

@ @<Check that |NoCache| and...@>=
process(t, p, `<fake>keep</fake>`, Request{NoCache: true})
if got := readFile(t, svg); got != "P:keep:Q" {
	t.Errorf("NoCache did not rebuild: %q", got)
}
writeFile(t, svg, "SENTINEL")
process(t, p, `<fake cache="no">keep</fake>`, Request{})
if got := readFile(t, svg); got != "P:keep:Q" {
	t.Errorf(`cache="no" did not rebuild: %q`, got)
}

@ The attributes the pipeline consumes must not survive into the output.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestConsumedAttributesAreRemoved(t *testing.T) {
	p := newProcessor(t, t.TempDir())
	doc := process(t, p, `<fake cmd="cp" cache="no" width="50">x</fake>`, Request{})
	img := htmldoc.Elements(doc, "img")[0]
	for _, k := range []string{"cmd", "cache"} {
		if htmldoc.HasAttr(img, k) {
			t.Errorf("attribute %q leaked into the output", k)
		}
	}
}

@ An updater registered for a tag takes over from the default. This one turns
the node into a \.{<figure>} holding the source and the URI.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestUpdatersSelectPerTag(t *testing.T) {
	p := newProcessor(t, t.TempDir())
	req := Request{Updaters: Updaters{ByTag: map[string]UpdateNodeFunc{
		"fake": func(doc, node *htmldoc.Node, uri, content string) {
			htmldoc.Rename(node, "figure")
			htmldoc.SetTextContent(node, content+"|"+uri)
		},
	}}}
	doc := process(t, p, `<fake>src</fake>`, req)
	fig := htmldoc.Elements(doc, "figure")
	if len(fig) != 1 {
		t.Fatalf("got %d figure elements, want 1", len(fig))
	}
	if got := htmldoc.TextContent(fig[0]); !strings.HasPrefix(got, "src|/images/") {
		t.Errorf("figure text = %q", got)
	}
}

@ Many nodes at once is the whole point of the port. These two tests are
meant to be run under \.{go test -race}: forty different nodes in one page,
then eight pages racing on the same source, which must not fight over one
file.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestConcurrentNodes(t *testing.T) {
	p := newProcessor(t, t.TempDir())
	var b strings.Builder
	for i := range 40 {
		b.WriteString("<fake>n" + strconv.Itoa(i) + "</fake>")
	}
	doc := process(t, p, b.String(), Request{})
	if n := len(htmldoc.Elements(doc, "img")); n != 40 {
		t.Fatalf("got %d img elements, want 40", n)
	}
	if n := len(htmldoc.Elements(doc, "pre")); n != 0 {
		t.Errorf("got %d error nodes, want none", n)
	}
}

@ @(render_test.go@>=
func TestConcurrentIdenticalSources(t *testing.T) {
	root := t.TempDir()
	p := newProcessor(t, root)
	done := make(chan struct{})
	for range 8 {
		go func() {
			defer func() { done <- struct{}{} }()
			doc, _ := htmldoc.Parse(strings.NewReader(`<fake>same</fake>`), p.RawTags()...)
			p.Process(context.Background(), doc, Request{BaseURI: "/p.html"})
		}()
	}
	for range 8 {
		<-done
	}
	svg := filepath.Join(root, "images", "cp"+crcOf("same")+".svg")
	if got := readFile(t, svg); got != "P:same:Q" {
		t.Errorf("svg = %q", got)
	}
}

@* Tests with real engines.
The engines are real programs, so the remaining tests run only where they are
installed, and skip themselves elsewhere.
@<Test helpers@>=
func requireCommand(t *testing.T, name string) {
	t.Helper()
	if _, err := exec.LookPath(name); err != nil {
		t.Skipf("%s is not installed", name)
	}
}

@ MetaPost, end to end, with the characters the gumbo-based original lost: a
\.{<} in a comparison and an \.{\&} joining two strings.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestMetaPostEndToEnd(t *testing.T) {
	requireCommand(t, "mpost")
	p, err := New(Options{Root: t.TempDir(), Renderers: []*Renderer{MetaPost}, Logger: quietLogger()})
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	const src = `<metapost width="100">
beginfig(1)
  n:=3;
  if n<5: draw fullcircle scaled 2cm; fi
  label("n = "&decimal n, origin);
endfig;
</metapost>`
	doc, err := htmldoc.Parse(strings.NewReader(src), p.RawTags()...)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	@<Process |doc| and check that it became one MetaPost SVG@>
}

@ @<Process |doc| and check that it became one MetaPost SVG@>=
if err := p.Process(context.Background(), doc, Request{BaseURI: "/p.html"}); err != nil {
	t.Fatal(err)
}
if pre := htmldoc.Elements(doc, "pre"); len(pre) > 0 {
	t.Fatalf("mpost failed: %s", htmldoc.TextContent(pre[0]))
}
img := htmldoc.Elements(doc, "img")
if len(img) != 1 {
	t.Fatalf("got %d img elements, want 1", len(img))
}
uri, _ := htmldoc.Attr(img[0], "src")
if !strings.HasPrefix(uri, "/images/mpost") || !strings.HasSuffix(uri, ".svg") {
	t.Errorf("src = %q", uri)
}
name := strings.TrimSuffix(strings.TrimPrefix(uri, "/images/"), ".svg")
if got := readFile(t, p.svgPath(name)); !strings.Contains(got, "<svg") {
	t.Errorf("output is not an SVG: %.60q", got)
}

@ Graphviz, with a \.{cmd} attribute that must pick \.{neato}.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestGraphvizEndToEnd(t *testing.T) {
	requireCommand(t, "dot")
	p, err := New(Options{Root: t.TempDir(), Renderers: []*Renderer{Graphviz}, Logger: quietLogger()})
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	doc, err := htmldoc.Parse(strings.NewReader(`<graphviz cmd="neato">digraph{a->b->c}</graphviz>`), p.RawTags()...)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if err := p.Process(context.Background(), doc, Request{BaseURI: "/p.html"}); err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if pre := htmldoc.Elements(doc, "pre"); len(pre) > 0 {
		t.Fatalf("dot failed: %s", htmldoc.TextContent(pre[0]))
	}
	if s, _ := htmldoc.Attr(htmldoc.Elements(doc, "img")[0], "src"); !strings.HasPrefix(s, "/images/neato") {
		t.Errorf("src = %q, want the cmd attribute to pick neato", s)
	}
}

@ Finally, a failing engine must leave the page standing, with the log in red
where the image would have been, and the log must name the engine.
@(render_test.go@>=
func TestEngineFailureBecomesVisibleError(t *testing.T) {
	requireCommand(t, "mpost")
	p, err := New(Options{Root: t.TempDir(), Renderers: []*Renderer{MetaPost}, Logger: quietLogger()})
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	const src = `<metapost>this is not metapost at all (((</metapost>`
	doc, err := htmldoc.Parse(strings.NewReader(src), p.RawTags()...)
	if err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	if err := p.Process(context.Background(), doc, Request{BaseURI: "/p.html"}); err != nil {
		t.Fatal(err)
	}
	pre := htmldoc.Elements(doc, "pre")
	if len(pre) != 1 {
		t.Fatalf("got %d error nodes, want 1", len(pre))
	}
	if style, _ := htmldoc.Attr(pre[0], "style"); style != "color:red" {
		t.Errorf("style = %q", style)
	}
	if got := htmldoc.TextContent(pre[0]); !strings.Contains(got, "mpost") {
		t.Errorf("error text does not name the engine: %q", got)
	}
}

@* Index.
